1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:10,999
The release page says v0.3.0. The build
machine runs git describe and prints v0.2.0,

2
00:00:11,039 --> 00:00:16,448
dash one, dash a commit ID. Support
asks which version customers have.

3
00:00:16,488 --> 00:00:19,561
Your CTO asks: which one is the version?

4
00:00:19,901 --> 00:00:24,600
Both are. A release and a tag are different
objects made by different programs.

5
00:00:24,640 --> 00:00:29,158
The release was created on the platform
for a tag that did not exist, so the

6
00:00:29,198 --> 00:00:34,196
platform made a tag, at whatever commit was
at the tip of the default branch at that

7
00:00:34,236 --> 00:00:39,234
second. Nobody chose that commit with git
tag. And the build's git describe does not

8
00:00:39,274 --> 00:00:42,533
count that kind of tag. Keep
those two strings in mind.

9
00:00:42,573 --> 00:00:43,613
You'll print both.

10
00:00:45,452 --> 00:00:49,158
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:49,198 --> 00:00:54,132
You learned tags in Part 3: lightweight,
a bare name for a commit, and annotated,

12
00:00:54,172 --> 00:00:58,799
with its own object, tagger and message.
They live under refs/tags/, and git

13
00:00:58,839 --> 00:01:03,711
describe reads them. In video 115 you
sorted "release" into the column of GitHub

14
00:01:03,751 --> 00:01:08,869
objects and "tag" into the column of Git
data. This video is the place where the two

15
00:01:08,909 --> 00:01:13,598
columns touch, and where a platform
action writes Git data that nobody typed.

16
00:01:13,938 --> 00:01:20,896
One section, 15.12. Two replays:
labs/ch15/tag-vs-release.sh, in which a plain git

17
00:01:20,936 --> 00:01:28,065
tag on a bare repository stands in for what
the platform does, and the replay of Lab

18
00:01:28,105 --> 00:01:34,295
22.2. Then the walkthrough of Lab 22.2,
Part B, on a practice repository.

19
00:01:34,635 --> 00:01:40,387
Labels: git tag -a adds a tag object and a
ref. git push origin <tag> publishes it.

20
00:01:40,427 --> 00:01:46,110
gh release create is a state change on
GitHub and, when the tag does not exist, on

21
00:01:46,150 --> 00:01:51,972
the repository's refs. The textbook labels
it 🔴 DANGEROUS without --verify-tag and 🟡

22
00:01:52,012 --> 00:01:55,879
CAUTION with it, and I always
show it with --verify-tag.

23
00:01:57,719 --> 00:01:59,132
After this video you can:

24
00:01:59,472 --> 00:02:04,213
Separate the tag, which is Git data, from
the Release, which is a GitHub object.

25
00:02:04,553 --> 00:02:09,370
Explain how creating a release can create
a tag on the server, and of which kind.

26
00:02:09,710 --> 00:02:12,984
Explain why git describe and
the release page can disagree.

27
00:02:13,324 --> 00:02:16,455
Cut a release from an annotated
tag that was pushed first.

28
00:02:16,795 --> 00:02:20,217
Say what an immutable release
adds, as the section states it.

29
00:02:22,057 --> 00:02:25,618
In one sentence: a tag is a
Git ref that names a commit.

30
00:02:25,658 --> 00:02:30,699
A release is a GitHub record that points
at a tag name and adds a title, notes,

31
00:02:30,739 --> 00:02:31,728
files and flags.

32
00:02:32,068 --> 00:02:37,526
The Git side you know. The GitHub side,
from the documentation: "Releases are based

33
00:02:37,566 --> 00:02:43,089
on Git tags." A release adds a title, notes,
uploaded assets and three flags: draft,

34
00:02:43,129 --> 00:02:46,467
pre-release, latest. Managing
releases needs Write.

35
00:02:46,807 --> 00:02:50,489
Inside .git: a release leaves
no trace in any clone.

36
00:02:50,529 --> 00:02:53,138
The tag does, and only after a fetch.

37
00:02:53,478 --> 00:02:57,895
Now the mechanism of the hook, on one
branch. A tag was made on purpose, and

38
00:02:57,935 --> 00:03:00,182
afterwards main moved on by one commit.

39
00:03:00,522 --> 00:03:05,145
Creating a release can create the tag.
The REST reference describes a field,

40
00:03:05,185 --> 00:03:10,236
target_commitish, as the value "that
determines where the Git tag is created from".

41
00:03:10,276 --> 00:03:15,021
It is unused if the tag already exists,
and it defaults to the default branch.

42
00:03:15,061 --> 00:03:20,051
The GitHub CLI says the same about itself
in its help text: "If a matching git tag

43
00:03:20,091 --> 00:03:25,143
does not yet exist, one will automatically
get created from the latest state of the

44
00:03:25,183 --> 00:03:26,125
default branch."

45
00:03:26,465 --> 00:03:30,885
Of which kind is that tag? The
textbook is careful here, and so am I.

46
00:03:30,925 --> 00:03:35,798
That a tag created by the release API
is lightweight is an inference, marked

47
00:03:35,838 --> 00:03:41,164
unverified: the CLI help tells you to create
annotated tags yourself, and a lab has

48
00:03:41,204 --> 00:03:46,465
you check with git cat-file -t. In the
replay, a lightweight tag stands in for it.

49
00:03:46,805 --> 00:03:52,240
Why would the kind matter? Because git
describe uses annotated tags only, unless you

50
00:03:52,280 --> 00:03:57,454
give --tags. Annotated tags are meant for
releases, lightweight tags for private

51
00:03:57,494 --> 00:04:02,017
labels. A lightweight tag has no tag
object, no tagger and no message.

52
00:04:02,357 --> 00:04:06,886
The prevention has an order. Create
and push the annotated tag first.

53
00:04:06,926 --> 00:04:12,383
Then create the release with --verify-tag,
which aborts the release if the tag does

54
00:04:12,423 --> 00:04:17,946
not already exist. The CLI's own help gives
the procedure: "To create a release from

55
00:04:17,986 --> 00:04:23,244
an annotated git tag, first create one
locally with git, push the tag to GitHub,

56
00:04:23,284 --> 00:04:28,740
then run this command." --notes-from-tag
takes the release notes from the annotated

57
00:04:28,780 --> 00:04:29,668
tag's message.

58
00:04:30,008 --> 00:04:35,355
Generated notes. They list merged pull
requests and contributors, and are configured

59
00:04:35,395 --> 00:04:40,229
in .github/release.yml, where labels
and authors can be excluded or grouped.

60
00:04:40,269 --> 00:04:44,591
Their quality is the quality of your
pull request titles and labels.

61
00:04:44,931 --> 00:04:49,785
Immutable releases, generally available
since the twenty-eighth of October 2025.

62
00:04:49,825 --> 00:04:54,618
Once published: the tag is locked to its
commit and cannot be deleted while the

63
00:04:54,658 --> 00:04:59,207
release exists. Assets cannot be changed.
The tag name can never be reused.

64
00:04:59,247 --> 00:05:03,979
And a signed release attestation is
generated, checked with gh release verify.

65
00:05:04,019 --> 00:05:08,139
The documented order is: create a
draft, attach the assets, publish.

66
00:05:08,479 --> 00:05:13,306
Two more items the textbook marks unverified,
which I repeat as unverified: what

67
00:05:13,346 --> 00:05:18,172
happens to a release when its tag is
deleted, and whether immutable releases are

68
00:05:18,212 --> 00:05:22,917
limited to certain plans, are not stated
in the documentation the course read.

69
00:05:23,257 --> 00:05:28,257
The recovery, when the wrong thing has
happened: decide which commit is the version.

70
00:05:28,297 --> 00:05:31,976
Then either describe with --tags,
or replace the tag properly.

71
00:05:32,016 --> 00:05:36,955
And the root-cause box adds that a new
annotated tag under a new name is safer than

72
00:05:36,995 --> 00:05:37,615
moving one.

73
00:05:40,955 --> 00:05:47,159
git tag -a then git push origin v0.2.0:
locally, a tag ref and a tag object.

74
00:05:47,199 --> 00:05:54,060
On the remote, both are created. On GitHub,
the tag is listed and no release exists.

75
00:05:54,100 --> 00:06:00,469
gh release create v0.2.0 --verify-tag
--generate-notes: nothing changes in Git

76
00:06:00,509 --> 00:06:05,891
anywhere, and GitHub gains a release
pointing at the existing tag.

77
00:06:09,231 --> 00:06:15,609
gh release create v0.3.0 when no such tag
exists: your clone is unchanged until you

78
00:06:15,649 --> 00:06:21,718
fetch. The remote gains a tag created
from the default branch or from --target.

79
00:06:21,758 --> 00:06:28,136
GitHub has a release, and a tag you did not
make. gh release delete: the tag stays,

80
00:06:28,176 --> 00:06:32,389
unless --cleanup-tag is given,
and the release is gone.

81
00:06:32,729 --> 00:06:37,318
Quick quiz on that last row, from
memory now that the table is gone.

82
00:06:37,358 --> 00:06:41,403
gh release delete, no other
option: what happens to the tag?

83
00:06:41,443 --> 00:06:44,194
A: deleted too. B: it stays. Your answer?

84
00:06:49,034 --> 00:06:52,613
B. The release is GitHub's record,
the tag is Git data, and it stays.

85
00:06:54,453 --> 00:06:58,085
The textbook's analogy: a tag is
the mark stamped on a casting.

86
00:06:58,125 --> 00:07:02,398
A release is the catalogue page for
it, with a description and a download.

87
00:07:02,438 --> 00:07:05,487
The page can be rewritten
without touching the metal.

88
00:07:05,827 --> 00:07:10,513
The analogy breaks in one dangerous place:
asking for a page for a mark that does

89
00:07:10,553 --> 00:07:15,356
not exist makes the platform stamp the mark
for you, on whatever is at the front of

90
00:07:15,396 --> 00:07:15,881
the line.

91
00:07:16,221 --> 00:07:20,927
So the discipline is the order of work.
Stamp first, by hand, on the casting you

92
00:07:20,967 --> 00:07:25,672
chose. Then ask for the page, and tell
the catalogue to refuse if it cannot find

93
00:07:25,712 --> 00:07:26,325
your stamp.

94
00:07:26,665 --> 00:07:31,697
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper: write
the three commands of a release, in that

95
00:07:31,737 --> 00:07:32,724
order. I'll wait.

96
00:07:37,564 --> 00:07:43,894
git tag -a, then git push origin with the
tag name, then gh release create with

97
00:07:43,934 --> 00:07:44,942
--verify-tag.

98
00:07:46,782 --> 00:07:52,601
On the left, Git. The first tag goes
through a tag object, db476fe, which has a

99
00:07:52,641 --> 00:07:58,683
tagger, a date and a message, to commit
bf7889c. The second tag points directly at

100
00:07:58,723 --> 00:08:01,427
commit 89837fa: no object in between.

101
00:08:01,767 --> 00:08:06,717
On the right, the platform. Two release
records, each pointing at a tag name.

102
00:08:06,757 --> 00:08:08,986
Nothing on the right is in a clone.

103
00:08:09,326 --> 00:08:15,581
At the bottom, the hook as two commands.
git describe counts from the nearest

104
00:08:15,621 --> 00:08:22,204
annotated tag: v0.2.0, one commit later.
With --tags it sees the lightweight one:

105
00:08:22,244 --> 00:08:22,776
v0.3.0.

106
00:08:23,116 --> 00:08:27,923
Read the root-cause line: the release was
created for a tag that did not exist, so

107
00:08:27,963 --> 00:08:31,587
the platform created the tag at
the tip of the default branch.

108
00:08:35,927 --> 00:08:40,579
You create an annotated tag on the tip of
the branch and push it, the way a release

109
00:08:40,619 --> 00:08:41,597
is cut on purpose.

110
00:08:43,937 --> 00:08:49,403
One tag was pushed. How many lines will
git ls-remote --tags print for it?

111
00:08:49,743 --> 00:08:56,207
Two lines for one tag: the tag object, and
with the ^{} suffix the commit it points

112
00:08:56,247 --> 00:08:56,442
at.

113
00:08:56,782 --> 00:09:02,050
Then Asha merges one more commit. Then
someone creates a release v0.3.0 on the

114
00:09:02,090 --> 00:09:07,359
platform, without creating a tag first.
A plain git tag on the bare repository

115
00:09:07,399 --> 00:09:10,830
stands in for what the
platform does at that point.

116
00:09:11,170 --> 00:09:16,785
The server has a tag that your clone has
never seen: git tag --list still shows one.

117
00:09:16,825 --> 00:09:22,104
And for the new tag there is one line
this time: no tag object, only a ref that

118
00:09:22,144 --> 00:09:27,154
names a commit. Predict: after a fetch,
how many tags will your clone list?

119
00:09:27,194 --> 00:09:28,231
Say it out loud.

120
00:09:33,071 --> 00:09:35,641
Two. The fetch brings
the commit and the tag.

121
00:09:35,981 --> 00:09:42,308
Two kinds, in one listing. v0.2.0 has object
type tag, a tagger and its own message.

122
00:09:42,348 --> 00:09:48,068
v0.3.0 has object type commit, an empty
tagger, and the subject shown is the

123
00:09:48,108 --> 00:09:48,751
commit's.

124
00:09:51,091 --> 00:09:56,980
The tip of main carries the tag v0.3.0.
What does plain git describe print?

125
00:09:57,320 --> 00:10:05,422
v0.2.0-1-g89837fa: one commit
after the last annotated tag.

126
00:10:05,462 --> 00:10:08,182
With --tags: v0.3.0.

127
00:10:08,522 --> 00:10:13,030
Both are true statements about the same
commit. That's the build log and the release

128
00:10:13,070 --> 00:10:14,058
page from the hook.

129
00:10:16,898 --> 00:10:21,947
An annotated tag is an object of its own:
type tag, and inside it the object it

130
00:10:21,987 --> 00:10:25,683
names, the type, the tag name,
the tagger and the message.

131
00:10:26,023 --> 00:10:31,067
What a release would point at: the tag
ref, of type tag, peeled to a commit.

132
00:10:31,107 --> 00:10:35,416
On GitHub the next step is the
release command with --verify-tag.

133
00:10:35,756 --> 00:10:40,175
Say what each line creates and where. The
tag: an object and a ref in your clone.

134
00:10:40,215 --> 00:10:44,744
The push: the same two on the server.
According to the documentation the tag is now

135
00:10:44,784 --> 00:10:48,268
listed on the repository's tags
page, and no release exists yet.

136
00:10:48,608 --> 00:10:53,831
gh release create: a GitHub object and
nothing in Git, because --verify-tag found

137
00:10:53,871 --> 00:10:59,290
the tag. In the browser, the releases page
shows the title, the notes taken from the

138
00:10:59,330 --> 00:11:02,734
tag message, and the source
archives GitHub attaches.

139
00:11:02,774 --> 00:11:07,673
Finally git ls-remote --tags: predict
the number of lines before you run it.

140
00:11:08,013 --> 00:11:12,993
The lab then has you create a release first,
for a tag that does not exist, look at

141
00:11:13,033 --> 00:11:16,743
the tag the platform made with
git cat-file -t, and repair it.

142
00:11:16,783 --> 00:11:21,037
Record what you find: it is the
observation behind the unverified note.

143
00:11:22,877 --> 00:11:24,525
Five mistakes to watch for.

144
00:11:24,865 --> 00:11:29,069
Creating a release in the web interface
for a version that has no tag.

145
00:11:29,109 --> 00:11:34,162
Root cause: the platform creates the tag,
from the default branch unless a target is

146
00:11:34,202 --> 00:11:36,891
given, at a commit nobody
chose with git tag.

147
00:11:37,231 --> 00:11:41,756
Deriving a version with git describe
and wondering why a tag is ignored.

148
00:11:41,796 --> 00:11:46,448
Root cause: git describe uses annotated
tags only, unless --tags is given.

149
00:11:46,788 --> 00:11:50,886
Deleting a release to "undo" it
and assuming the tag is gone.

150
00:11:50,926 --> 00:11:55,974
Root cause: gh release delete leaves
the tag unless --cleanup-tag is given.

151
00:11:56,314 --> 00:12:00,939
Expecting release notes, assets or the
"latest" flag in a clone or a mirror.

152
00:12:00,979 --> 00:12:05,051
Root cause: a release is a GitHub
object; only the tag is Git data.

153
00:12:05,391 --> 00:12:10,508
Moving a published tag to fix a wrong
release. Root cause: clones that fetched the

154
00:12:10,548 --> 00:12:14,470
old tag keep it; a new annotated
tag under a new name is safer.

155
00:12:16,310 --> 00:12:21,448
Now, out of the lab. A workflow builds a
container image for an inference service

156
00:12:21,488 --> 00:12:26,371
whenever a tag matching v* is pushed,
and derives the version string with git

157
00:12:26,411 --> 00:12:31,293
describe. A product manager creates
"v1.9.0" in the web interface on a Friday

158
00:12:31,333 --> 00:12:36,599
evening. The tag lands on whatever main was
at that second, the workflow fires, and

159
00:12:36,639 --> 00:12:41,905
the version string inside the artifact is
wrong, because the tag is lightweight and

160
00:12:41,945 --> 00:12:45,805
git describe reports the previous
annotated tag plus a count.

161
00:12:46,145 --> 00:12:49,702
The textbook names three
controls that close the gap.

162
00:12:49,742 --> 00:12:55,405
A tag ruleset that restricts who may create
v* tags, which is three videos from now.

163
00:12:55,445 --> 00:13:01,039
--verify-tag in every release script. And
immutable releases for anything you ship.

164
00:13:01,379 --> 00:13:06,186
The team adds a fourth, procedural one,
which is this video's lab in one line: the

165
00:13:06,226 --> 00:13:10,974
release engineer creates and pushes the
annotated tag, and the release is created

166
00:13:11,014 --> 00:13:11,447
from it.

167
00:13:13,287 --> 00:13:17,391
Your turn. Do Lab 22.2, "A release
from an annotated tag", in

168
00:13:17,431 --> 00:13:23,029
lab-manual/m22-merge-methods-releases.md.
Before each git ls-remote --tags, predict

169
00:13:23,069 --> 00:13:28,532
the number of lines and what each names.
Before the failure scenario, predict the

170
00:13:28,572 --> 00:13:33,966
object type of the tag the platform will
create and what git describe will print

171
00:13:34,006 --> 00:13:36,412
afterwards, with and without --tags.

172
00:13:36,752 --> 00:13:43,644
The challenge is Exercise 22.5, "The
release that does not contain its fix", in

173
00:13:43,684 --> 00:13:46,101
exercises/m19-m25-github.md.

174
00:13:47,941 --> 00:13:51,239
Question 227 of the CTO question bank:

175
00:13:53,579 --> 00:13:58,292
Answer out loud. A strong answer puts each
of the two in its layer and says what

176
00:13:58,332 --> 00:14:03,044
travels with a clone. It describes the
sequence that makes the platform create a

177
00:14:03,084 --> 00:14:07,855
tag, where that tag is placed, and why a
build's version string can then disagree

178
00:14:07,895 --> 00:14:12,667
with the release page. For prevention it
gives the order of operations, the flag,

179
00:14:12,707 --> 00:14:17,419
and at least one platform control, and it
is honest about what the documentation

180
00:14:17,459 --> 00:14:18,310
does not state.

181
00:14:20,150 --> 00:14:25,406
Let's land this. A tag is Git's, a release
is GitHub's, and the order of work keeps

182
00:14:25,446 --> 00:14:26,555
them in agreement.

183
00:14:26,895 --> 00:14:28,554
You should now be able to say:

184
00:14:28,894 --> 00:14:33,835
A tag is a ref, lightweight or annotated;
a release is a GitHub record that points

185
00:14:33,875 --> 00:14:34,686
at a tag name.

186
00:14:35,026 --> 00:14:39,339
Creating a release for a tag that does not
exist makes the platform create the tag,

187
00:14:39,379 --> 00:14:41,909
from the default branch
unless a target is given.

188
00:14:42,249 --> 00:14:46,828
git describe counts annotated tags only,
so it can disagree with the release page.

189
00:14:47,168 --> 00:14:52,754
The order is: annotated tag, push,
then gh release create --verify-tag.

190
00:14:53,094 --> 00:14:57,698
An immutable release locks the tag to its
commit, fixes the assets, and adds an

191
00:14:57,738 --> 00:14:58,403
attestation.

192
00:15:00,243 --> 00:15:03,345
Read section 15.12 of Chapter 15.

193
00:15:03,685 --> 00:15:08,117
You can now cut a release on the commit
you chose. Practise with Lab 22.2.

194
00:15:08,157 --> 00:15:13,013
Next: rulesets, layering and bypass. Until
then, look at the state first and type

195
00:15:13,053 --> 00:15:14,947
second. See you in the next one.
